  <h1 class="page-heading">About</h1>
  <div class="about-layout">
    <img src="/images/about-portrait.jpg" alt="Portrait of Bart van Dam">
    <div class="about-body">

<p>I'm drawn to the technical side of photography: understanding light, controlling the camera, and squeezing every bit of sharpness out of an image. That curiosity has me regularly browsing sites like The Digital Picture, DXOMark, and PhotonsToPhotos to compare cameras and lenses, reading up on image processing, and watching videos on getting the most sharpness out of a shot.</p>

<p>My interest in microscopy comes from my background as an experimental physicist, where I used optical microscopy to study diffraction-limited materials — materials smaller than the wavelength of light itself. These materials interact with and alter light in measurable ways, through properties like polarization, wavelength shift, decay time, and quantum yield.</p>

<p>That research background left me with a soft spot for the old lab tools of the trade. I still have an old Olympus BH microscope at home, complete with an adapter for mounting an analog camera to take stills — and I've since had fun tinkering with it, adding automation, and experimenting with focus stacking and image processing.</p>

<p>At their core, microscopy and photography rely on the same principles — but the way you work with light is where they diverge. In microscopy, the light is yours to shape: brightfield, darkfield, phase contrast, polarization, fluorescence, and more. In nature photography, you work with whatever light the day gives you — a cloudy sky won't change a micrograph much, but it will completely shift the contrast, tone, and noise in a bird photo.</p>

<p>The other key difference is depth of field. In microscopy, it's typically far narrower than the subject itself, which is why focus stacking is often essential to build a single sharp image.</p>
